<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chartres</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Autricum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chartres is a city on the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eure">Eure</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Gallo-Roman Autricum, chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnutes">Carnutes</a>. It later became a seat of the counts of Blois and a centre of learning in the 11th and 12th centuries. Its cathedral, rebuilt after a fire of 1194 with outstanding stained glass, is one of the finest Gothic churches and a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chartres.jpg" alt="Chartres" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludvig14 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chartres_DJI_0352a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Rollo’s failed attack on <b>Chartres</b> in 911, King Charles the Simple of West Francia granted him lands around Rouen in return for defending the Seine against other raiders and accepting baptism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gothic architecture began in the Île-de-France with Abbot Suger's rebuilding of the abbey church of Saint-Denis in the 1140s and spread from cathedrals such as <b>Chartres</b>, Reims and Amiens across Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune was borrowed from a Catholic French song mocking the Huguenot prince Louis of Condé's failed siege of <b>Chartres</b> in 1568, so a satire of one Protestant prince became a tribute to another.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li><li><blockquote>A former Protestant who had converted to Catholicism, Henry was crowned at <b>Chartres</b> because Reims was held by the Catholic League.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rollo's Vikings, who had besieged <b>Chartres</b> in West Francia since spring, were defeated when a relief army arrived.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carnutes lived between the Seine and the Loire, around <b>Autricum</b> (Chartres) and Cenabum (Orléans).</blockquote><a href="/state/carnutes">Carnutes</a></li></ul></section>
